# For each pair, choose ONE label.
# 
#   same      The two submissions make the SAME argument, and the second adds no
#             substantive point the first does not already make. Wording may
#             differ freely. A form letter with a different signature, a different
#             town, or one inserted sentence of personal context is `same`.
# 
#   variant   Clearly derived from a common source, but one of them adds a real
#             substantive point of its own (a new objection, a new piece of
#             evidence, a different requested remedy).
# 
#   distinct  Two independently made arguments, even if they reach the same
#             conclusion. Two people who both say "this rule is too weak" in their
#             own words are DISTINCT: that is public opinion, not a campaign.
# 
#   unusable  One or both texts are too garbled, truncated or empty to judge.
# 
# If you are torn between `same` and `distinct`, choose `distinct`. Merging two
# real arguments erases someone from the public record; splitting a campaign only
# overstates diversity.
# 
# SECOND SAMPLE, stratified over MinHash Jaccard rather than cosine.
# The first sample was drawn over cosine bands from 0.80 up, which left the
# MinHash threshold sitting on a 0.445-wide plateau with no labelled pair in
# it. These 40 sit inside that plateau. Same rubric, same tie-break.
# Guide: labels/HOW_TO_LABEL.md
{"pair": 1027, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.6406, "cosine": 0.9085, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2869", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3417", "_i": 2530, "_j": 3005, "a_text": "As an advocate for our national parks, communities, public health and climate, I write to urge you to strengthen the proposed supplemental rule, Standards of Performance for New, Reconstructed, and Modified Sources and Emissions Guidelines for Existing Sources. As you know, methane is a powerful greenhouse gas and is often leaked and vented during oil and gas operations, degrading air quality, driving climate change, threatening public health, and harming the natural conditions of our national parks. While this updated supplemental rule improves upon the standards previously proposed in November 2021, there are three key aspects that need to be strengthened before EPA finalizes the proposal: 1. Completely eliminate the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities. 2. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making the standards applicable to more tanks. 3. Ensure that approved monitoring technologies and data are accessible to all so that communities and individuals can fully engage in the proposed Super Emitter Response Program. Thank you for your continued attention to curbing methane emissions, something we must do quickly if we are to stem further, more catastrophic climate impacts.", "b_text": "As an advocate for our national parks, communities, public health and climate, I write to urge you to strengthen the proposed supplemental rule, Standards of Performance for New, Reconstructed, and Modified Sources and Emissions Guidelines for Existing Sources. As you know, methane is a powerful greenhouse gas and is often leaked and vented during oil and gas operations, degrading air quality, driving climate change, threatening public health, and harming the natural conditions of our national parks. While this updated supplemental rule improves upon the standards previously proposed in November 2021, there are three key aspects that need to be strengthened before EPA finalizes the proposal: 1. Completely eliminate the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities. 2. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks by making the standards applicable to more tanks. 3. Ensure that approved monitoring technologies and data are accessible to all so that communities and individuals can fully engage in the proposed Super Emitter Response Program. Thank you for your continued attention to curbing methane emissions, something we must do quickly if we are to stem further, more catastrophic climate impacts. Havent big gas, oil &amp; other chemical companies done enough damage to our planet. Driving down back roads in their tanker trucks with a hose dragging along the ground to dispel toxic chemicals that made it into our drinking water an"}
{"pair": 1016, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5078, "cosine": 0.8246, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2511", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4028", "_i": 2172, "_j": 3415, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future I tried to put my money where my mouth is. I drive a 2009 Toyota prius. I am a member of a community solar farm. I am investigating the possibilities of geothermal. I do my part. You need to do yours. Sincerely, Mary Sweeney Lynn, MA 01902 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/6056.txt[2/21/2023 2:32:42 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely yours, Louise-Lora Somlyo \u00e2 \u0303\u00ae\u00ef \u0327\u00f0\u0178...\u2039\u00ef \u0327 Sincerely, Louise Lora Somlyo Portland, ME 04102"}
{"pair": 1021, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.6094, "cosine": 0.835, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1221", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1239", "_i": 1011, "_j": 1029, "a_text": "Dear EPA Methane Docket email, MY GRAND CHILDREN WILL ONLY BE IN THEIR 30'S IN 2050. WE ARE COUNTING ON YOU TO STOP THE GROSS POLLUTION THAT IS CAUSING AND WORSENING CLIMATE CHANGE. WE ARE COUNTING ON YOU TO HAVE THE RESOLVE TO REQUIRE FOSSIL FUEL COMPANIES TO PIVOT TO MORE CLEAN ENERGY PRODUCTION. THEY HAVE THE RESOURCES, INTELLIGENCE, AND MONEY TO DO SO, AND SHOULD NOT BE ALLOWED TO CONTINUE POLLUTING AT OUR EXPENSE, USING SUBSIDIES FROM MY TAX DOLLARS. THE QUICKER THEY ARE REQUIRED TO PIVOT FROM POLLUTING ENERGY TO CLEAN ENERGY, THE BETTER OFF WE WILL ALL BE, THEM INCLUDED, AND THE MORE ECONOMIC WE WILL ALL BE IN THE LONG RUN. SHORT TERM EASE OF PROFIT IS NOT WORTH THE LONG TERM COSTS TO OUR HEALTH, WELL BEING, AND GOVERNMENT COFFERS AS WE ATTEMPT TO REMEDIATE FOSSIL FUEL POLLUTION. I applaud the EPA for taking action to curb oil and gas methane pollution on behalf of the 12.5 million people living in our country who live near oil and gas drilling operations and all of us who face impacts from climate change. The rule in its current form will do a lot to curb oil and gas methane pollution, and I specifically support federal methane standards for existing equipment and requiring zero-emitting pneumatic controllers at all new and existing sites. Thank you for also improving on the Obama Administration's oil and gas standards by, for example, including emission reduction requirements for liquids unloading events, regulating methane at storage tanks, and increasing the frequen", "b_text": "Dear EPA Methane Docket email, I believe that, in order to have healthy humans, we must maintain a healthy planet. Thank you very much for working towards that! I applaud the EPA for taking action to curb oil and gas methane pollution on behalf of the 12.5 million people living in our country who live near oil and gas drilling operations and all of us who face impacts from climate change. The rule in its current form will do a lot to curb oil and gas methane pollution, and I specifically support federal methane standards for existing equipment and requiring zero\u2010emitting pneumatic controllers at all new and existing sites. Thank you for also improving on the Obama Administration's oil and gas standards by, for example, including emission reduction requirements for liquids unloading events, regulating methane at storage tanks, and increasing the frequency of leak monitoring at many oil and gas wells. However, I believe the rule must be strengthened in many ways. EPA must go even further by increasing mandatory leak inspections to monthly for all wells and compressor stations and by eliminating the exemption from regular monitoring from those for wells that are calculated to emit below three tons per year of methane. We urge EPA to further study advanced LDAR monitoring as one way of increasing the scope of its monitoring requirements. In addition, flaring is a major nuisance and source of pollution for local communities near oil and gas operations. It must also be strictly lim"}
{"pair": 1030, "band": "0.70-0.80", "jaccard": 0.7266, "cosine": 0.8859, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1640", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1964", "_i": 1306, "_j": 1630, "a_text": "Perez, JuanB NORMAN R NORVELLE < > Comments To: From: Norman R. Norvelle, M.S. Date: January 10, 2023 Subject: EPA\u2019s Supplemental Proposal to Reduce Pollution from O&G \u2013 Comments I completely agree with your Supplemental Proposal to Reduce Pollution from the Oil and Natural Gas Industry to Fight the Climate Crisis and Protect Public Health: Overview. This needs to be done to reduce pollution from the oil and gas industry. I worked 20 years in the energy industry (oil & gas and Coal) as an industrial chemist and 20 years in state government as a public health scientist, safety consultant and environmental health scientist area manager. I live in Farmington, NM. In 1994 I founded the Four Corners Oil and Gas Conference. New Mexico is one of the major oil and gas producers in the nation. In 2014 NASA discovered a giant hotspot over the Four Corners area (mainly the San Juan Basin). We have the highest concentration of methane in the nation. As you know, methane traps 80 times more heat in the atmosphere than the same amount of carbon dioxide. Outside of carbon dioxide it is the next most serious greenhouse gas (GHG). I have witnessed the damage done to the Four Corners area by the oil and gas industry and was part of it. I spent much of my time and career doing monitoring, remediation and clean-ups of contaminated sites. I am concerned and have comments on four areas I feel need more attention: \uf0b7 Plugging long-term idle wells that are abandoned or orphaned \uf0b7 Training for staff m", "b_text": "Jan 10 23, 08:27p Norman Norvelle 5053275910 p.1 To: From: Norman R. Norvelle, M.S. Date: January 10, 2023 Subject: EPA's Supplemental Proposal to Reduce Pollution from O&G - Comments ,- \u2022 \u2022\u2022 ,:,, \u00b7, 0 --,;i1\u20444z,.,,,..,I.i ,Jq~nnpli:;J~ly.~gr!!y:,'Wi~h your. S\u03bcpplem,ental Proposal t.o R~d\u03bcce Polluti,9Tl,from.the Oil_ gl),9 Natural Gas Industry to Fight the Climate Crisis and Protect Public Health: Overview. This needs to be done to reduce pollution from the oil and gas industry. I worked 20 years in the energy industry (oil & gas and Coal) as an industrial chemist and 20 years in state government as a public health scientist, safety consultant and enviro_nmental health scientist area manager. I live in Fannington, NM. In 1994 I founded the Four Corners Oil and Gas Conference. New Mexico is one of the major oil and gas producers in the nation. rn 2014 NASA discovered a giant hotspot over the Four Comers area (mainly the San Juan Basin). We have the highest concentration of methane in the nation. As you know, methane traps 80 times more heat in the atmosphere than the same amount of carbon dioxide. Outside of carbon dioxide it is the next most serious greenhouse gas (GHG). I have witnessed the damage done to the Four Corners area by the oil and gas industry and was part of it. I spent much of my time and career doing monitoring, remediation and clean-ups of contaminated sites. I am concerned and have comments on four areas I feel need more attention: \u2022 Plugging long-term idle w"}
{"pair": 1035, "band": "0.80-0.90", "jaccard": 0.8672, "cosine": 0.9742, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2778", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4038", "_i": 2439, "_j": 3425, "a_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, on behalf of Katherine Quinn Asher < > As a mom, I worry about the future we are leaving for our children. Methane leaks from the oil and gas industry are a potent and dangerous greenhouse gas that is changing our climate. Moms work hard to protect their children and set them up for a bright future. The last thing we want for our kids is a future of drought, wildfire, increased insect-borne disease, sea level rise, and extreme weather events. For this reason, I support the current proposal to update the rules regarding oil and gas drilling to reduce methane emissions. Despite previous efforts, the level of methane has increased and so much more needs to be done. As the UN panel on climate change has warned us, we must act quickly and boldly. This proposal should be finalized and additional safeguards should be added as well. More than 17 million people in the U.S. live within a half mile of oil and gas production and are exposed to public health threats as a result. These individuals are disproportionately communities of color. Here in my state of Colorado, we have banned routine flaring. If we can do it here, it can be done nationwide. Please limit flaring to only those times when it is necessary for maintenance or an emergency. I can\u2019t protect my kids from air pollution by myself so I need the EPA to adopt strong, comprehensive methane safeguards. Rules that will protect the health of kids today and the future that they will inherit from us. Si", "b_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, As a Mom and Grandma I worry about the future we are leaving for our children. Methane leaks from the oil and gas industry are a potent and dangerous greenhouse gas that is changing our climate. Moms work hard to protect their children and set them up for a bright future. The last thing we want for our kids is a future of drought, wildfire, increased insect-borne disease, sea level rise, and extreme weather events. For this reason, I support the current proposal to update the rules regarding oil and gas drilling to reduce methane emissions. Despite previous efforts, the level of methane has increased and so much more needs to be done. As the UN panel on climate change has warned us, we must act quickly and boldly. This proposal should be finalized and additional safeguards should be added as well. More than 17 million people in the U.S. live within a half mile of oil and gas production and are exposed to public health threats as a result. These individuals are disproportionately communities of color. Here in my state of Colorado, we have banned routine flaring. If we can do it here, it can be done nationwide. Please limit flaring to only those times when it is necessary for maintenance or an emergency. I can\u00e2\u20acTMt protect my kids from air pollution by myself so I need the EPA to adopt strong, comprehensive methane safeguards. Rules that will protect the health of kids today and the future that they will inherit from us. Sincerely, Karen Montgomery"}
{"pair": 1004, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3828, "cosine": 0.8054, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2894", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3540", "_i": 2555, "_j": 3122, "a_text": "Thanks for proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions, but it needs to be stronger. We need to limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. We also need to strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. We need to provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program.", "b_text": "Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program. thank you for this opportunity to comment on this issue."}
{"pair": 1029, "band": "0.70-0.80", "jaccard": 0.7891, "cosine": 0.9398, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4032", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4045", "_i": 3419, "_j": 3432, "a_text": "\u00bf From: K Stuhr < > To: < Date: 2/14/2023 12:36:00 AM Subject: Act urgently and cut methane emissions now. Docket #: EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ ----- Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additional strain on threatened species through accelerating climate change. Cutting methane pollution will also have profound effects on millions of people across the U.S. experiencing record heat. Slowing today\u00e2\u20acTMs unprecedented rate of warming can help avert our most acute climate risks, including megafires and extreme weather. New oil and gas sector pollution limits will also help prevent greater suffering from severe health impacts caused by smog and exposure to cancer-causing toxic emissions, a particular threat to communities of color and those living near facilities. Now, the EPA must move quickly to ena", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additional strain on threatened species through accelerating climate change. Cutting methane pollution will also have profound effects on millions of people across the U.S. experiencing record heat. Slowing today&rsquo;s unprecedented rate of warming can help avert our most acute climate risks, including megafires and extreme weather. New oil and gas sector pollution limits will also help prevent greater suffering from severe health impacts caused by smog and exposure to cancer-causing toxic emissions, a particular threat to communities of color and those living near facilities. Now, the EPA must move quickly to enact solutions for pollution and finalize the strongest possible safeguards&mdash;such as eliminating pollution from flaring, requiring regular monitoring at smaller wells with leak-pr"}
{"pair": 1039, "band": "0.90-1.01", "jaccard": 1.0, "cosine": 1.0, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0314", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0669", "_i": 129, "_j": 467, "a_text": "As pro-life Christians, we want the air we breathe to be safe for our children. But leaks in our oil/gas infrastructure spew toxic pollutants that put God's creation and our families - pregnant mothers and the unborn - in harm's way. Unborn children close to leaks are at higher risk of birth defects, and children attending school near leaks have higher rates of cancer and asthma. That's why Christians are calling on the EPA to ensure that stronger regulations and inspections will cut this pollution from both existing and new leaks, particularly from low-producing wells. Our kids deserve better.", "b_text": "As pro-life Christians, we want the air we breathe to be safe for our children. But leaks in our oil/gas infrastructure spew toxic pollutants that put God's creation and our families - pregnant mothers and the unborn - in harm's way. Unborn children close to leaks are at higher risk of birth defects, and children attending school near leaks have higher rates of cancer and asthma. That's why Christians are calling on the EPA to ensure that stronger regulations and inspections will cut this pollution from both existing and new leaks, particularly from low-producing wells. Our kids deserve better."}
{"pair": 1034, "band": "0.70-0.80", "jaccard": 0.7188, "cosine": 0.9234, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0494", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1305", "_i": 298, "_j": 1095, "a_text": "Dear EPA Methane Docket email, Stop kowtowing to rich party donors and actually Subsidizing polluting filthy fossil fuels, trying to have it both ways which is impossible! This Climate Catastrophe is an Emergency, delays will inevitably lead to even more Natural Disasters such as tornadoes, wildfires, and floods, than we are already experiencing! Money should be spent on monitoring safety, with the aim of finally switching to renewable energy. I applaud the EPA for taking action to curb oil and gas methane pollution on behalf of the 12.5 million people living in our country who live near oil and gas drilling operations and all of us who face impacts from climate change. The rule in its current form will do a lot to curb oil and gas methane pollution, and I specifically support federal methane standards for existing equipment and requiring zero-emitting pneumatic controllers at all new and existing sites. Thank you for also improving on the Obama Administration's oil and gas standards by, for example, including emission reduction requirements for liquids unloading events, regulating methane at storage tanks, and increasing the frequency of leak monitoring at many oil and gas wells. However, I believe the rule must be strengthened in many ways. EPA must go even further by increasing mandatory leak inspections to monthly for all wells and compressor stations and by eliminating the exemption from regular monitoring from those for wells that are calculated to emit below three tons", "b_text": "Dear EPA Methane Docket email, Methane is a small component of the atmosphere, but a very effective greenhouse gas. Please increase regulation on methane emissions from oil drilling. I applaud the EPA for taking action to curb oil and gas methane pollution on behalf of the 12.5 million people living in our country who live near oil and gas drilling operations and all of us who face impacts from climate change. The rule in its current form will do a lot to curb oil and gas methane pollution, and I specifically support federal methane standards for existing equipment and requiring zero\u2010emitting pneumatic controllers at all new and existing sites. Thank you for also improving on the Obama Administration's oil and gas standards by, for example, including emission reduction requirements for liquids unloading events, regulating methane at storage tanks, and increasing the frequency of leak monitoring at many oil and gas wells. However, I believe the rule must be strengthened in many ways. EPA must go even further by increasing mandatory leak inspections to monthly for all wells and compressor stations and by eliminating the exemption from regular monitoring from those for wells that are calculated to emit below three tons per year of methane. We urge EPA to further study advanced LDAR monitoring as one way of increasing the scope of its monitoring requirements. In addition, flaring is a major nuisance and source of pollution for local communities near oil and gas operations. It mus"}
{"pair": 1013, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4141, "cosine": 0.8853, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0470", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0477", "_i": 274, "_j": 281, "a_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, Methane pollution drives climate change and threatens all of us. In the U.S., oil and gas production is the largest industrial source of methane pollution. Thanks to the Environmental Protection Agency\u2019s new proposed rule, we now have long awaited opportunity to restore regulations to reduce methane pollution. The rule promises to cut methane emissions by 41 million tons. Because methane acts as a super-pollutant in the near- term, these reductions will help buy the U.S. time to continue working to mitigate the climate crisis and protect people and wildlife. While EPA's proposed methane safeguards are an important step in the right direction, they do not go far enough. EPA must strengthen their proposed rule to protect public health and the climate, through a supplemental rulemaking or other action, in order to: 1. End the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities, as states like Colorado and New Mexico have done already. 2. Require regular monitoring at all smaller, high-polluting and leak-prone wells, and work to incorporate emission monitoring results generated by community groups and other third parties into its standards. We cannot miss out on this opportunity to cut methane pollution, safeguard public health and wildlife, and act on climate. That means strengthening the agency\u2019s proposed rules to finalize the most comprehensive methane safeguards that finally rein in methane emissions from oil and gas operatio", "b_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, Methane pollution is threatening our communities and wildlife by driving climate change. In the U.S., oil and gas production is the largest industrial source of methane pollution. While EPA's proposed methane safeguards are an important step in the right direction, they do not go far enough and the EPA must strengthen their proposed rule to protect public health and the climate, through a supplemental rulemaking or other action, in order to: 1. End the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities, as states like Colorado and New Mexico have done already. 2. Require regular monitoring at all smaller, high-polluting and leak-prone wells, and work to incorporate emission monitoring results generated by community groups and other third parties into its standards. Thank you for your consideration, D Alford file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2002%201.19.22%2012826/16377.txt[1/26/2022 7:44:14 PM]"}
{"pair": 1012, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4609, "cosine": 0.8854, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2571", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4029", "_i": 2232, "_j": 3416, "a_text": "From: Thomas Keenan ( ) Sent You a Personal Message < > To: Date: 2/8/2023 10:39:37 PM Subject: : I urge you to enact strong methane safeguards ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Dear U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) , Having two family members with asthma, watching our native pines all rapidly dying on our hillsides and on the valley floor, here in Northern Idaho, seeing the leaves still on the trees when we finally get temperatures cold enough to normally cause them to drop, but instead they freeze to the trees, looking out the window and seeing no snow or hardly any anymore, looking into my children's and grand children's eyes and seeing the fear when the subject of our stranger weather and dying trees comes up nearly every day now, with neighbors and friends, knowing how many pollinators and birds are now gone, seeing pictures of dying coral, and flooded communities, I mean come on? are we that greedy power hungry, and ignorant, that we?ll ignore the signs all around us and cash in while leaving our kids and grandkids hung out to dry (fry)? We will go the way of the dinosaurs, dumber and one more mistake by the great divine web, a wrong branch of evolution! Nature doesn't deserve our carelessness Right now, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has an opportunity to confront the climate crisis head-on and protect the health and safety of communities like mine by finalizing strong, comprehens", "b_text": "\u00bf From: Krissa Takatsuka ( ) Sent You a Personal Message To: < Date: 2/14/2023 1:47:22 AM Subject: : I urge you to enact strong methane safeguards ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ ------------ Dear U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) , Right now, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has an opportunity to confront the climate crisis head-on and protect the health and safety of communities like mine by finalizing strong, comprehensive safeguards to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. Every year, the oil and gas industry releases sixteen million metric tons of methane into our atmosphere. Methane is a potent climate pollutant with more than 80 times the warming power of carbon dioxide in the near-term. This pollution has real and devastating impacts in communities across the country, including my own. Last fall, the EPA proposed new protections to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. While this is an important step in the right direction, there is more work to be done to ensure that communities like mine are protected. I am counting on EPA to issue a supplemental proposal that would, if finalized, a ban on routine flaring, eliminate potential loopholes that allow smaller wells with leak-prone equipment to forgo regular inspections, require oil and gas operators to use non-emitting pneumatic equipment across the entire sector, and empower community groups to monitor emissions at the sites i"}
{"pair": 1019, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5469, "cosine": 0.9103, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2970", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3665", "_i": 2621, "_j": 3239, "a_text": "My name is Kayla Calkins, and I live in Portland, OR]. I\u2019m writing to express support for the updated draft rule to reduce methane pollution from oil and gas wells. The harmful environmental and health effects of this extremely potent greenhouse gas are well documented, and it I would like to thank the EPA for its responsiveness to concerns from environmental and clean energy advocates expressed during the first open comment period. In particular, the inclusion of fugitive methane monitoring for small wells was an important gap to address from the previous draft rule. I encourage the EPA to strengthen requirements for community involvement in state implementation of the rule, and to require methane inspection data to be made publicly available. This will ensure proper accountability on behalf of all stakeholders on an issue that can have far A truly robust methane pollution standard would ban routine gas flaring by the oil industry and limit flaring to only highly specific cases for which there is no alternative. We\u2019re at a critical moment for capping planet-warming emissions before we see the worst effects of climate change. The proposed draft rule, combined with the methane fee recently passed in the Inflation Reduction Act, is essential for avoiding those tipping points. Having worked in the auto insurance industry for years, I am aware of the role oil and gas as emitted by vehicles as well as how the extraction of these materials emit to great consequence. In seeking to r", "b_text": "My name is Joanna, and I live in Northampton, Massachusetts. I\u2019m writing to express support for the updated draft rule to reduce methane pollution from oil and gas wells. The harmful environmental and health effects of this extremely potent greenhouse gas are well documented, and it I would like to thank the EPA for its responsiveness to concerns from environmental and clean energy advocates expressed during the first open comment period. In particular, the inclusion of fugitive methane monitoring for small wells was an important gap to address from the previous draft rule. I encourage the EPA to strengthen requirements for community involvement in state implementation of the rule, and to require methane inspection data to be made publicly available. This will ensure proper accountability on behalf of all stakeholders on an issue that can have far A truly robust methane pollution standard would ban routine gas flaring by the oil industry and limit flaring to only highly specific cases for which there is no alternative. We\u2019re at a critical moment for capping planet-warming emissions before we see the worst effects of climate change. The proposed draft rule, combined with the methane fee recently passed in the Inflation Reduction Act, is essential for avoiding those tipping points. We're already feeling the devastating impacts of climate change. I lived in California for a few years, and poor air quality caused by annual wildfires is part of what brought me back to the East coa"}
{"pair": 1010, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4766, "cosine": 0.9582, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1857", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2678", "_i": 1523, "_j": 2339, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. Cutting methane emissions is one of the most important things we can do to slow climate change in the short term--which is the span of time let to us in which we can still prevent the most catastrophic impacts of climate change. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Felicity Devlin Tacoma, WA 98406 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ain/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/9222.txt[1/23/2023 11:54:14 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, We know that methane emissions are a significant problem!!! I read about it and am very concerned about the very dangerous warming of our atmosphere. The storms, fires, huge rains snd flooding are costing lives and millions of dollars. PLEASE focus on cutting emissions! Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Anne Cox La Jolla, CA 92037 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/7182.txt[2/21/2023 2:33:00 PM]"}
{"pair": 1009, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4375, "cosine": 0.9583, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2633", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4051", "_i": 2294, "_j": 3438, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Your actions to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry are appreciated. They are a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. Please do all you can to strengthen and implement common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Lorrie Epling Pacifica, CA 94044 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/6464.txt[2/21/2023 2:32:49 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Scott Heinze Mount Vernon, NY 10552"}
{"pair": 1014, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5234, "cosine": 0.7395, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2730", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2881", "_i": 2391, "_j": 2542, "a_text": "From: David Baker < > To: Date: 2/4/2023 10:19:00 PM Subject: ---------------------------------------------------- Dear Sir or Madame: I am deeply concerned about the impacts of air pollution, combined with the effects of climate change, harming our health and making our communities unsafe, especially for children. Please finalize a strong and comprehensive methane rule to prevent methane leaks during production and transport, and also for abandoned and/or depleted wells. Methane is a potent greenhouse gas, accelerating climate change and harming our health and the wellbeing of our communities. Strengthening this rule will help protect our air, our climate and our health. Therefore, this rule must: * Enact a ban on routine flaring; * Require regular inspections at the hundreds of thousands of small wells; * Follow through on their commitment to community monitoring programs and work to incorporate emission monitoring results generated by community groups; and * Require the use of non-emitting pneumatic controllers across the entire oil and gas sector. David Baker Warning: electronic transmissions are not secure. Do not send important personal information such as social security numbers, account numbers, birth dates, driver\u2019s licenses, or passports by way of electronic transmission to this office. If it is important that such information be provided, either do so by regular mail, landline telephone, or, as to account numbers, please provide the last four digits only. file:///W", "b_text": "I am deeply concerned about the impacts of air pollution, combined with the effects of climate change, harming our health and making our communities unsafe, especially for children. Please finalize a strong and comprehensive methane rule to prevent methane leaks during production and transport, and also for abandoned and/or depleted wells. Methane is a potent greenhouse gas, accelerating climate change and harming our health and the wellbeing of our communities. Strengthening this rule will help protect our air, our climate and our health. Therefore, this rule must: Enact a ban on routine flaring; Require regular inspections at the hundreds of thousands of small wells; Follow through on their commitment to community monitoring programs and work to incorporate emission monitoring results generated by community groups; and Require the use of non-emitting pneumatic controllers across the entire oil and gas sector."}
{"pair": 1033, "band": "0.70-0.80", "jaccard": 0.7422, "cosine": 0.8295, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2489", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2780", "_i": 2150, "_j": 2441, "a_text": "To the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), The Sierra Club submits the following 9,579 digital signatures on the behalf of our members and supporters with the following comment language and personalized messages: \"Right now, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has an opportunity to confront the climate crisis head-on and protect the health and safety of communities like mine by finalizing strong, comprehensive safeguards to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. Every year, the oil and gas industry releases sixteen million metric tons of methane into our atmosphere. Methane is a potent climate pollutant with more than 80 times the warming power of carbon dioxide in the near-term. This pollution has real and devastating impacts in communities across the country, including my own. Last fall, the EPA proposed new protections to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. While this is an important step in the right direction, there is more work to be done to ensure that communities like mine are protected. I am counting on EPA to issue a supplemental proposal that would, if finalized, a ban on routine flaring, eliminate potential loopholes that allow smaller wells with leak-prone equipment to forgo regular inspections, require oil and gas operators to use non-emitting pneumatic equipment across the entire sector, and empower community groups to monitor emissions at the sites in our own backyards. There is no time to waste, and we cannot miss out o", "b_text": "From: Allen Altman ( ) Sent You a Personal Message < > To: Date: 1/8/2023 11:20:31 PM Subject: : I urge you to enact strong methane safeguards ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ -------------------- Dear U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) , Right now, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has an opportunity to confront the climate crisis head-on and protect the health and safety of communities like mine by finalizing strong, comprehensive safeguards to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. Every year, the oil and gas industry releases sixteen million metric tons of methane into our atmosphere. Methane is a potent climate pollutant with more than 80 times the warming power of carbon dioxide in the near-term. This pollution has real and devastating impacts in communities across the country, including my own. Last fall, the EPA proposed new protections to cut methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. While this is an important step in the right direction, there is more work to be done to ensure that communities like mine are protected. I am counting on EPA to issue a supplemental proposal that would, if finalized, a ban on routine flaring, eliminate potential loopholes that allow smaller wells with leak-prone equipment to forgo regular inspections, require oil and gas operators to use non-emitting pneumatic equipment across the entire sector, and empower community groups to monitor emissions at t"}
{"pair": 1006, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3359, "cosine": 0.7719, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2051", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2585", "_i": 1717, "_j": 2246, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Cannot the methane burned off at oil wells be put to use if captured? Not all wells seem to spout it. If used, it would displace other fossil fuels and decrease pollution by sparing us the double whammy of burning flared gas plus fossil fuels. Please act to solve the flared gas problem and to decrease methane pollution in any other ways reasonably possible. Sincerely, John Tzucker MD Sincerely, John Tzucker Carmel, IN 46033 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...vio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/10390.txt[2/1/2023 2:29:24 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. This is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Todd Gutmann Cupertino, CA 95014 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2010%202.16.22%2010571/48.txt[2/22/2023 9:52:54 AM]"}
{"pair": 1003, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3984, "cosine": 0.8217, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2512", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2604", "_i": 2173, "_j": 2265, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. I am afraid that global warming will melt the permafrost, which would release large amounts of methane, which in turn would increase warming. A feed-back loop that could be a \"tipping point\" from which we could not recover is the worst-case scenario that we must do our best to prevent. Sincerely, Martha P Cottle Chilmark, MA 02535 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/17957.txt[2/21/2023 2:34:09 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Valerie Lockard Columbus, OH 43220 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2010%202.16.22%2010571/10199.txt[2/22/2023 9:56:24 AM]"}
{"pair": 1037, "band": "0.80-0.90", "jaccard": 0.8359, "cosine": 0.9447, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0866", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1146", "_i": 657, "_j": 936, "a_text": "Dear EPA, I am writing to express my support for reducing methane emissions in the natural gas and oil supply chains and requiring methane measurement from all components of each supply chain. I urge you to discontinue reporting methane emissions as 100-year carbon dioxide emissions equivalency, and to require all measurements across the entire oil and natural gas supply chains to be provided in actual methane emissions (in pounds and or in tons). The rule should also include all methane emissions, including the unburned methane emitted from the exhaust smokestacks from natural gas-fired compressor units, generators, and boilers located at many compressor station facilities. Reducing methane pollution is one of the most effective things we can do to mitigate climate change in the short run. Thank you for making these critical rule improvements and enforcing stronger reporting of methane emissions.", "b_text": "Dear EPA, I am writing to express my support for reducing methane emissions in the natural gas and oil supply chains and requiring methane measurement from all components of each supply chain. I urge you to discontinue reporting methane emissions as 100-year carbon dioxide emissions equivalency, and to require all measurements across the entire oil and natural gas supply chains to be provided in actual methane emissions (in pounds and or in tons). The rule should also include all methane emissions, including the unburned methane emitted from the exhaust smokestacks from natural gas-fired compressor units, generators, and boilers located at many compressor station facilities. Methane and all hazardous air pollutants emitted from natural gas and oil facilities must be fully reported. Regardless of claimed reported methane emissions, facilities should be required to perform actual baseline methane measurements annually during peak load periods. Reducing methane pollution is one of the most effective things we can do to mitigate climate change in the short run. Thank you for making these critical rule improvements and enforcing stronger reporting of methane emissions. Dear EPA, I am writing to express my support for reducing methane emissions in the natural gas and oil supply chains and requiring methane measurement from all components of each supply chain. I urge you to discontinue reporting methane emissions as 100-year carbon dioxide emissions equivalency, and to require all m"}
{"pair": 1001, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3203, "cosine": 0.8881, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2912", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3470", "_i": 2563, "_j": 3057, "a_text": "Thank you for proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions, but as an environmental engineer, I believe it needs to be stronger. Here are some suggestions for strengthening the rule. Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program. Thanks,", "b_text": "First, I'd like to thank the EPA for at least proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions...but the rule needs to be stronger. The EPA needs to limit the flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. And the EPA needs to address emissions from storage tanks. The EPA also needs to provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program."}
{"pair": 1011, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4219, "cosine": 0.7819, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2536", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2572", "_i": 2197, "_j": 2233, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Please think of the future of this planet. There is no planet B, and we need to take care of the only home we have. Thank you, Becky Hayob Sincerely, Becky Hayob Minneapolis, MN 55448 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/7938.txt[2/21/2023 2:33:12 PM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Why would you let this go forwsrd? Why poison anumore areas? Its craziness. Why arent you fining the burning Merhane burning lose across old Oil Fielfs? If they do not pay up, and cap them, its time for jail of the Top Brass. QUIT SCREWING AROUND WITY PRECIOUS TIME. PEOPLE GROM THE RIGHT WANT TO SHUT YOU DOWN. I DONT. YOU PLAY A VITAL PART IN AMERICA. PLEASE TAKE CARE OF THESE PROBLEMS NOW! Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Stephani Mohr Bondurant, IA 50035 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...io%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2009%202.15.23%2020080/3488.txt[2/21/2023 2:32:17 PM]"}
{"pair": 1002, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3672, "cosine": 0.7727, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1948", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2017", "_i": 1614, "_j": 1683, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Very good start on methane control, but we most certainly need more action than contemplated. I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Robert Lyman Minneapolis, MN 55407 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/11204.txt[1/23/2023 11:55:00 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Further to the below; I am a retired petroleum engineer and electric wireline engineer in Texas. I had smelled a pipeline leak near Ft Stockton for years, TCEQ was doing nothing. And, flaring should be prohibited except in emergencies and limited cleanup after completions. If there isn't a pipeline for gas instead of flaring, maybe wells should be shut in! ----------- Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Kevin Hartley Houston, TX 77028 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/15382.txt[1/23/2023 11:56:37 AM]"}
{"pair": 1036, "band": "0.80-0.90", "jaccard": 0.8594, "cosine": 0.9426, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2772", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4031", "_i": 2433, "_j": 3418, "a_text": "From: Alexistori Gonzalez < > To: Date: 1/9/2023 1:28:00 AM Subject: Act urgently and cut methane emissions now. Docket #: EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additional strain on threatened species through accelerating climate change. Cutting methane pollution will also have profound effects on millions of people across the U.S. experiencing record heat. Slowing today\u2019s unprecedented rate of warming can help avert our most acute climate risks, including megafires and extreme weather. New oil and gas sector pollution limits will also help prevent greater suffering from severe health impacts caused by smog and exposure to cancer-causing toxic emissions, a particular threat to communities of color and those living near facilities. Now, the EPA must move quickly to e", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additional strain on threatened species through accelerating climate change. Cutting methane pollution will also have profound effects on millions of people across the U.S. experiencing record heat. Slowing today&rsquo;s unprecedented rate of warming can help avert our most acute climate risks, including megafires and extreme weather. New oil and gas sector pollution limits will also help prevent greater suffering from severe health impacts caused by smog and exposure to cancer-causing toxic emissions, a particular threat to communities of color and those living near facilities. Now, the EPA must move quickly to enact solutions for pollution and finalize the strongest possible safeguards&mdash;such as eliminating pollution from flaring, requiring regular monitoring at smaller wells with leak-pr"}
{"pair": 1000, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3047, "cosine": 0.9124, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1791", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2145", "_i": 1457, "_j": 1811, "a_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, I just listened to a program on National Public Radio about the huge releases of methane as a result of the LNG industry and the shipping of same to other countries. It contributes hugely to climate change. Thank you for taking action to reduce methane emissions from the oil and gas industry. Your action is a big step in the right direction, and I encourage you to strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating and people across the United States are feeling the impact. Dramatically reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, highest-impact actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Stephanie Wellemeyer Berkeley, CA 94710 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...in/Equivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2007%2016049/15303.txt[1/23/2023 11:56:35 AM]", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Please strengthen the final rules to maximize emissions reductions. The climate crisis is accelerating, and people across the United States are feeling the effects. Reducing methane emissions is one of the quickest, largest-effect actions you can take to begin stemming the climate crisis today. Thank you for your consideration and for your action to cut methane emissions. I look forward to seeing how you will strengthen and implement these common-sense regulations in the near future. Sincerely, Sheldon Isaac Philadelphia, PA 19144 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...ivio%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%208%201.26.23%2020427/2802.txt[2/1/2023 2:26:26 PM]"}
{"pair": 1005, "band": "0.30-0.40", "jaccard": 0.3906, "cosine": 0.6849, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2222", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2226", "_i": 1887, "_j": 1891, "a_text": "Wisconsin DNR attaches the following comment letter on the Standards of Performance for New, Reconstructed, and Modified Sources and Emissions Guidelines for Existing Sources: Oil and Natural Gas Sector Climate Review.", "b_text": "Hi, I am submitting comments on behalf of Thermo Fisher Scientific in response to EPA's proposal: Standards of Performance for New, Reconstructed, and Modified Sources and Emissions Guidelines for Existing Sources: Oil and Natural Gas Sector Climate Review."}
{"pair": 1025, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.625, "cosine": 0.9065, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2826", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2877", "_i": 2487, "_j": 2538, "a_text": "Please limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Please strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Please provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program. Thank you.", "b_text": "Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program."}
{"pair": 1020, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5234, "cosine": 0.9516, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0507", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0606", "_i": 311, "_j": 404, "a_text": "Dear EPA Oil & Gas Docket, As a working mother, an asthmatic, and the mom of an asthmatic, I am deeply concerned with the health impacts of air pollution and climate change. That\u2019s why I was encouraged to learn that the EPA has proposed rules to cut methane and other harmful air pollutants from new and existing oil and gas operations. This is an important first step to address the impacts of methane pollution. I urge you to ensure that these rules are strong and comprehensive. They must cover all sources of oil and gas operations including smaller, leak-prone wells and limit routine flaring, both of which are outsized sources of methane emissions. Quickly and significantly reducing methane pollution is one of the best steps we can take to slow the rate of climate change now and it help clean up the air to protect our children\u2019s health. Federal methane rules will establish vital protections for all children across the nation, especially for states that have failed to enact meaningful oil and gas methane protections at the state level. Please ensure that the EPA passes comprehensive methane rules to protect our children\u2019s health and future. The complete focus on reducing climate change is the single action that will best help the most people, particularly children, now and in the future. Sincerely, K Malik 923 Gregory Ave Wilmette, IL 60091 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Report%20Run%2002%201.19.22%2012826/12487.txt[1/26/2022 7:42:07 ", "b_text": "Dear EPA Oil & Gas Docket, As a parent and member of Moms Clean Air Force, I\u2019m deeply concerned with the health impacts of air pollution and climate change. That\u2019s why I was encouraged to learn that the EPA has proposed rules to cut methane and other harmful air pollutants from new and existing oil and gas operations. This is an important first step to address the impacts of methane pollution. That\u2019s why I urge you to ensure that these rules are strong and comprehensive. They must cover all sources of oil and gas operations including smaller, leak-prone wells and limit routine flaring, both of which are outsized sources of methane emissions.. Quickly and significantly reducing methane pollution is one of the best levers we have to slow the rate of climate change now, and it will help clean up the air to protect children\u2019s health. Federal methane rules will establish vital protections for all children across the nation, especially for states that have failed to enact meaningful oil and gas methane protections at the state level. Please ensure that the EPA passes comprehensive methane rules to protect our children\u2019s health and future. Nothing is more important than leaving a habitable planet for our grandchildren (if any). My own kids are so distressed about the state of the planet that they are not planning to have children of their own. Sincerely, David Carter 144 Chisholm Trail Redlands, CA 92373 file:///W1818TDCEC026.aa.ad.epa.gov/...%20(Runs)/OAR-2021-0317/Reports/EPA_Repo"}
{"pair": 1017, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5781, "cosine": 0.8432, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0495", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1402", "_i": 299, "_j": 1192, "a_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, Methane pollution is threatening our communities and wildlife by driving climate change. In the U.S., oil and gas production is the largest industrial source of methane pollution. This pollution is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and cherished wildlife \u2014 and it\u2019s only getting worse. Thanks to the Environmental Protection Agency\u2019s new proposed rule, we now have an opportunity to restore regulations that rein in methane pollution. The rule promises to cut methane emissions by 41 million tons. Because methane acts as a super-pollutant in the near- term, these reductions will help buy the U.S. time to continue working to solve the climate crisis and protect people and wildlife from unavoidable impacts. The rule also will reduce volatile organic compound (VOC) emissions by 12 million tons and hazardous air pollution by 480,000 tons, which will help prevent lung disease and asthma, smog, and premature death. While EPA's proposed methane safeguards are an important step in the right direction, they do not go far enough, and must be strengthened in order to protect communities on the frontlines of oil and gas development, and confront the climate crisis head-on. EPA must strengthen their proposed rule to protect public health and the climate, through a supplemental rulemaking or other action, in order to: 1. End the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities, as states like Colo", "b_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, Methane pollution is threatening our communities and wildlife by driving climate change. In the U.S., oil and gas production is the largest industrial source of methane pollution. This pollution is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and cherished wildlife \u2014 and it\u2019s only getting worse. Thanks to the Environmental Protection Agency\u2019s new proposed rule, we now have an opportunity to restore regulations that rein in methane pollution. The rule promises to cut methane emissions by 41 million tons. Because methane acts as a super\u2010pollutant in the near\u2010term, these reductions will help buy the U.S. time to continue working to solve the climate crisis and protect people and wildlife from unavoidable impacts. The rule also will reduce volatile organic compound (VOC) emissions by 12 million tons and hazardous air pollution by 480,000 tons, which will help prevent lung disease and asthma, smog, and premature death. While EPA's proposed methane safeguards are an important step in the right direction, they do not go far enough, and must be strengthened in order to protect communities on the frontlines of oil and gas development, and confront the climate crisis head\u2010on. EPA must strengthen their proposed rule to protect public health and the climate, through a supplemental rulemaking or other action, in order to: 1. facilities, as states like Colorado and New Mexico have done already. End the wasteful and dangerous pract"}
{"pair": 1026, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.6328, "cosine": 0.9476, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3155", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3547", "_i": 2782, "_j": 3129, "a_text": "My name is Nicolas Benito Arcalas, and I'm from Goose Creek, South Carolina. I\u2019m writing to express support for the updated draft rule to reduce methane pollution from oil and gas wells. The harmful environmental and health effects of this extremely potent greenhouse gas are well documented, and it I would like to thank the EPA for its responsiveness to concerns from environmental and clean energy advocates expressed during the first open comment period. In particular, the inclusion of fugitive methane monitoring for small wells was an important gap to address from the previous draft rule. I encourage the EPA to strengthen requirements for community involvement in state implementation of the rule, and to require methane inspection data to be made publicly available. This will ensure proper accountability on behalf of all stakeholders on an issue that can have far A truly robust methane pollution standard would ban routine gas flaring by the oil industry and limit flaring to only highly specific cases for which there is no alternative. We\u2019re at a critical moment for capping planet-warming emissions before we see the worst effects of climate change. The proposed draft rule, combined with the methane fee recently passed in the Inflation Reduction Act, is essential for avoiding those tipping points. As a young person slated to face the consequences of my predecessors incredibly irresponsible treatment of our planet as I reach middle Once again, thank you for listening to environm", "b_text": "My name is Gabriella, and I live in Atlanta, GA. I\u2019m writing to express support for the updated draft rule to reduce methane pollution from oil and gas wells. The harmful environmental and health effects of this extremely potent greenhouse gas are well documented, and it I would like to thank the EPA for its responsiveness to concerns from environmental and clean energy advocates expressed during the first open comment period. In particular, the inclusion of fugitive methane monitoring for small wells was an important gap to address from the previous draft rule. I encourage the EPA to strengthen requirements for community involvement in state implementation of the rule, and to require methane inspection data to be made publicly available. This will ensure proper accountability on behalf of all stakeholders on an issue that can have far A truly robust methane pollution standard would ban routine gas flaring by the oil industry and limit flaring to only highly specific cases for which there is no alternative. We\u2019re at a critical moment for capping planet-warming emissions before we see the worst effects of climate change. The proposed draft rule, combined with the methane fee recently passed in the Inflation Reduction Act, is essential for avoiding those tipping points. Methane is such a potent greenhouse gas and organizations should be penalized for using it unnecessarily. As tracking Scope 1, 2, and 3 emissions becomes more prevalent and organizations have a better feel for t"}
{"pair": 1007, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4844, "cosine": 0.8792, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1214", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1385", "_i": 1004, "_j": 1175, "a_text": "In the United States, the oil and natural gas industry is the largest industrial source of methane emissions, emitting more methane than the total emissions of all greenhouse gases from 164 countries combined. The proposed EPA rule, which would reduce 41 million tons of methane emissions from 2023 to 2035, is a step in the right direction, but the EPA must go further. A steeper cut in methane emissions must be achieved to meet the Paris Accord. One way to do this is for the proposed rule to mandate regular leak monitoring requirements for all oil and gas wells, without exception, and to impose strict limits on routine flaring of gas at oil wells. The proposed rule states that well sites with estimated emissions of less than 3 tons per year must promptly conduct a survey (and perform repairs as needed) to demonstrate they are free of leaks or malfunctions but are not required to undertake ongoing monitoring. This is unacceptable. Small wells are a significant source of methane emissions, and must be subject to monitoring. Well sites, regardless of size, emit pollutants that are detrimental to public health, and therefore, should be subject to ongoing monitoring. The proposed rule states that sites with estimated emissions between 3 and 8 tons per year be monitored semi-annually, rather than quarterly. This is also unacceptable. They must be monitored quarterly so that leaks can be remediated quickly. The proposed rule also does not address abandoned and orphaned oil and gas we", "b_text": "Dear Sir or Madam, I tried my best to submit this comment on the web page, but while I could read other comments I did not see a link to make a comment. My comment: We are headed towards a climate catastrophe unless urgent action is taken. We will very soon see feedback loops that will make climate disasters a part of daily living. We are facing massive death and destruction in the future. One of the fasted ways to stop this is to control methane. The EPA\u2019s own website states the following facts: \u00b7 The oil and gas industry is a significant source of emissions of methane. \u00b7 Methane is a potent greenhouse gas with a global warming potential more than 25 times that of carbon dioxide. \u00b7 The the oil and gas industry is the largest industrial source of emissions of volatile organic compounds (VOCs), that contribute to the formation of ground-level ozone (smog), and include air toxics such as benzene, ethylbenzene, and n-hexane, VOC\u2019s are pollutants known, or suspected of, causing cancer and other serious health effects. \u00b7 One third of the warming from greenhouse gases occurring today is due to human-caused emissions of methane. \u00b7 In the United States, the oil and natural gas industry is the largest industrial source of methane emissions, emitting more methane than the total emissions of all greenhouse gases from 164 countries combined. The proposed EPA rule, which would reduce 41 million tons of methane emissions from 2023 to 2035, is a step in the right direction, but the EPA must"}
{"pair": 1032, "band": "0.70-0.80", "jaccard": 0.7969, "cosine": 0.9243, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4034", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4046", "_i": 3421, "_j": 3433, "a_text": "\u00bf From: Cree Maxson < > To: < Date: 2/14/2023 12:14:00 AM Subject: EPA\u00e2\u20acTMs updated methane rules are good for people and wildlife. Docket #: EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ ----------------------- Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additional strain on threatened species through accelerating climate change. Cutting methane pollution will also have profound effects on millions of people across the U.S. experiencing record heat. Slowing today\u00e2\u20acTMs unprecedented rate of warming can help avert our most acute climate risks, including megafires and extreme weather. New oil and gas sector pollution limits will also help prevent greater suffering from severe health impacts caused by smog and exposure to cancer-causing toxic emissions, a particular threat to communities of color and those living near facili", "b_text": "Dear Environmental Protection Agency, Cutting methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is the quickest, most cost effective way to slow the rate of climate change happening now and protect community and wildlife health. That is why I support the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) proposed updated rule to cut methane and other harmful pollutants from oil and gas operations across the country. My community has waited too long for bold action to cut methane, and we need these protections now more than ever. Reducing methane emissions is of the utmost importance for wildlife that continue to be negatively impacted by oil and gas development that fragments their habitat and puts additional strain on threatened species through accelerating climate change. Cutting methane pollution will also have profound effects on millions of people across the U.S. experiencing record heat. Slowing today&rsquo;s unprecedented rate of warming can help avert our most acute climate risks, including megafires and extreme weather. New oil and gas sector pollution limits will also help prevent greater suffering from severe health impacts caused by smog and exposure to cancer-causing toxic emissions, a particular threat to communities of color and those living near facilities. Now, the EPA must move quickly to enact solutions for pollution and finalize the strongest possible safeguards&mdash;such as eliminating pollution from flaring, requiring regular monitoring at smaller wells with leak-pr"}
