{"pair": 1024, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.6875, "cosine": 0.9398, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1419", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1437", "_i": 1209, "_j": 1227, "a_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, Oil and gas production is the largest industrial source of methane pollution In the U.S. This pollution is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and cherished wildlife \u2014 and it\u2019s only getting worse. Thanks to the Environmental Protection Agency\u2019s new proposed rule, we now have an opportunity to restore regulations that rein in methane pollution. While EPA's proposed methane safeguards are an important step in the right direction, they do not go far enough, and must be strengthened in order to protect communities on the frontlines of oil and gas development, and confront the climate crisis head\u2010on. EPA must strengthen their proposed rule to protect public health and the climate, through a supplemental rulemaking or other action, in order to: End the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities, as states like Colorado and 1. New Mexico have done already. Require regular monitoring at all smaller, high\u2010polluting and leak\u2010prone wells, and work to incorporate emission 2. monitoring results generated by community groups and other third parties into its standards. We cannot miss out on this opportunity to cut methane pollution, safeguard public health and wildlife, and act on climate. That means strengthening the agency\u2019s proposed rules to finalize the most comprehensive methane safeguards that finally rein in methane emissions from oil and gas operations. Thank you for your considera", "b_text": "Dear Administrator Regan, Methane pollution is threatening our communities and wildlife by driving climate change. In the U.S., oil and gas production is the largest industrial source of methane pollution. This pollution is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and cherished wildlife \u2014 and it\u2019s only getting worse. Thanks to the Environmental Protection Agency\u2019s new proposed rule, we now have an opportunity to restore regulations that rein in methane pollution. The rule promises to cut methane emissions by 41 million tons. Because methane acts as a super\u2010pollutant in the near\u2010 term, these reductions will help buy the U.S. time to continue working to solve the climate crisis and protect people and wildlife from unavoidable impacts. The rule also will reduce volatile organic compound (VOC) emissions by 12 million tons and hazardous air pollution by 480,000 tons, which will help prevent lung disease and asthma, smog, and premature death. While EPA's proposed methane safeguards are an important step in the right direction, they do not go far enough, and must be strengthened in order to protect communities on the frontlines of oil and gas development, and confront the climate crisis head\u2010on. EPA must strengthen their proposed rule to protect public health and the climate, through a supplemental rulemaking or other action, in order to: End the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas facilities, as states like Colorad"}
{"pair": 1022, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.6094, "cosine": 0.9451, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2734", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-4054", "_i": 2395, "_j": 3441, "a_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, Maralyn O'Brien, MoveOn Civic Action < > Enclosed are signatures and comments on the proposed rule to limit methane leaks from oil and gas drilling. Please consider the comments to include limiting the practice of flaring to strengthen the proposed methane regulations. This is a critical step to protect our communities and planet. Respectfully, Maralyn O\u2019Brien MoveOn Federal Affairs Signatories: \uf0b7 1", "b_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, Enclosed are signatures and comments on the proposed rule to limit methane leaks from oil and gas drilling. Please consider the comments to include limiting the practice of flaring to strengthen the proposed methane regulations. This is a critical step to protect our communities and planet. Respectfully, Maralyn O\u00e2\u20acTMBrien MoveOn Federal Affairs Signatories: *"}
{"pair": 1015, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5391, "cosine": 0.9294, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2885", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3081", "_i": 2546, "_j": 2711, "a_text": "thank you for proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions, but it needs to be stronger like: Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program.", "b_text": "Thank you for proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions, but it needs to be stronger! I strongly urge you to: Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program. Your attention to this most urgent matter would be much appreciated by all present & future generations of all species. Thank you Lydia Garvey Public Health Nurse"}
{"pair": 1038, "band": "0.90-1.01", "jaccard": 1.0, "cosine": 1.0, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0209", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0311", "_i": 25, "_j": 126, "a_text": "I urge you to strengthen and finalize the proposed rules to limit methane emissions from new and existing oil and gas sources. Reducing methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is one of the single fastest, most cost-effective ways to slow climate change. Over 12.5 million Americans live close enough to oil and gas operations to threaten their health with toxic air pollution that oil and gas production emits alongside methane. Stopping methane emissions will also protect frontline communities. In order to protect public health, slow climate change, and secure the planet for future generations, the administration must strengthen these rules to ensure frequent monitoring at all oil and gas infrastructure, including low-producing wells, and to prohibit flaring at oil wells. Thank you for the opportunity to comment.", "b_text": "I urge you to strengthen and finalize the proposed rules to limit methane emissions from new and existing oil and gas sources. Reducing methane pollution from the oil and gas industry is one of the single fastest, most cost-effective ways to slow climate change. Over 12.5 million Americans live close enough to oil and gas operations to threaten their health with toxic air pollution that oil and gas production emits alongside methane. Stopping methane emissions will also protect frontline communities. In order to protect public health, slow climate change, and secure the planet for future generations, the administration must strengthen these rules to ensure frequent monitoring at all oil and gas infrastructure, including low-producing wells, and to prohibit flaring at oil wells. Thank you for the opportunity to comment."}
{"pair": 1008, "band": "0.40-0.50", "jaccard": 0.4531, "cosine": 0.8529, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1040", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-1041", "_i": 830, "_j": 831, "a_text": "As a person of faith and conscience, I am writing to thank you for the recent rules on methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. The rules as proposed are an excellent start, but this critical moment of the climate crisis demands we do as much as possible immediately. The present moment is critical for arresting climate change. And methane, though it persists in the atmosphere for a shorter time than CO2, is far more potent at trapping heat. To ensure we decrease the potency of greenhouse gas in the atmosphere right now, we have to have the strongest possible safeguards against the release of methane. I am asking EPA to strengthen the proposed rule to end the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas operations, as states like Colorado and New Mexico have done already. I also ask EPA to require regular monitoring at all smaller, high-polluting and leak-prone wells. All faith traditions share a sacred calling to care for the earth and to care for those who are most vulnerable in our communities. Methane pollution causes deep and irreparable harm, polluting not only the land and the air, but also jeopardizing the health of children and those living in frontline communities. Methane is a potent greenhouse gas and it&rsquo;s responsible for a quarter of the warming that we are experiencing today. We know that far too much is leaking from oil and gas operations and contributing to the climate crisis while wasting this resource and endangering public ", "b_text": "As a person of faith and conscience, I am writing to thank you for the recent rules on methane pollution from the oil and gas industry. The rules as proposed are an excellent start, but this critical moment of the climate crisis demands we do as much as possible immediately. I am asking EPA to strengthen the proposed rule to end the wasteful and dangerous practice of routine flaring at oil and gas operations, as states like Colorado and New Mexico have done already. I also ask EPA to require regular monitoring at all smaller, high-polluting and leak-prone wells. All faith traditions share a sacred calling to care for the earth and to care for those who are most vulnerable in our communities. Methane pollution causes deep and irreparable harm, polluting not only the land and the air, but also jeopardizing the health of children and those living in frontline communities. Methane is a potent greenhouse gas and it&rsquo;s responsible for a quarter of the warming that we are experiencing today. We know that far too much is leaking from oil and gas operations and contributing to the climate crisis while wasting this resource and endangering public health. Our EPA must implement rules that will cut methane pollution from oil and gas operations 65 percent by 2025 while eliminating any loopholes around flaring and smaller wells that can contribute outsized methane pollution. It is EPA&rsquo;s obligation and moral opportunity to protect all Americans from harmful methane pollution. In "}
{"pair": 1018, "band": "0.50-0.60", "jaccard": 0.5859, "cosine": 0.9505, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-2939", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3796", "_i": 2590, "_j": 3365, "a_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, Methane pollution from the oil and gas sector is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and communities across the country. That is why we are counting on the Environmental Protection Agency to quickly finalize the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane and other harmful pollution from the oil and gas industry. Thank you for your time and efforts to protect our environment. Regards, Deborah Polatchek MS.HIS faculty- Southern New Hampshire University", "b_text": "Dear EPA Administrator Regan, Methane pollution from the oil and gas sector is accelerating the pace of climate change and harming the health of our families and communities across the country. That is why we are counting on the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to quickly finalize the strongest possible safeguards to cut methane and other harmful pollution from the oil and gas industry."}
{"pair": 1028, "band": "0.70-0.80", "jaccard": 0.7969, "cosine": 0.9755, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0195", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-0295", "_i": 11, "_j": 110, "a_text": "een Evangelical Environmental Network November 30, 2021 The Honorable Michael S. Regan Administrator of The United States Environmental Protection Agency 1200 Pennsylvania Avenue, N.W. Washington, DC 20460 24 E. Franklin Street New Freedom, PA 17349 717-850-3783 i @ Ref: Docket # EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317 Existing and New Source Standards For Reducing Methane and Toxins From the Oil/Gas Section Dear Administrator Regan, I am The Rev. Mitchell C. Hescox, President/C.E.O. of the Evangelical Environmental Network, and I live at 24 East Franklin Street, New Freedom, PA. As Evangelical Christians, we are people of the book, The Bible. Throughout Scripture, we are called to join God in pursuing justice. In the book of Amos, the prophet cries out: But let justice roll on like a river, and righteousness like a never-failing stream! (Amos 5:24 NIV). Additionally, as Jesus states in Matthew 25:40 (NIV), \u201cTruly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me. Our children are especially among those Jesus calls us to care for most. The old Sunday School song says it best: Jesus loves the little children, All the children of the world; Red, brown, yellow, black, and white, They are precious in His sight, Jesus loves the little children of the world. All of us would agree that our children are precious and must be defended from the threats imposed by known cancer causing toxins like benzene, VOCs that increase ozone levels, and methane tha", "b_text": "ee Evangelical Environmental Network November 30, 2021 The Honorable Michael S. Regan Administrator of The United States Environmental Protection Agency 1200 Pennsylvania Avenue, N.W. Washington, DC 20460 24 E. Franklin Street New Freedom, PA 17349 717-850-3783 rg Ref: Docket # EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317 Existing and New Source Standards For Reducing Methane and Toxins From the Oil/Gas Section Dear Administrator Regan, I am The Rev. Dr. Jeremy Summers, Board Chair of The Evangelical Environmental Network, and Faculty and Staff Member at Indiana Wesleyan University. I live in Marion, Indiana. As Evangelical Christians, we are people of the book, The Bible. Throughout Scripture, we are called to join God in pursuing justice. In the book of Amos, the prophet cries out: But let justice roll on like a river, and righteousness like a never-failing stream! (Amos 5:24 NIV). Additionally, as Jesus states in Matthew 25:40 (NIV), \u201cTruly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me. Our children are especially among those Jesus calls us to care for most, and as a Father of four children, this is personal. All of us would agree that our children are precious and must be defended from the threats imposed by known cancer causing toxins like benzene, VOCs that increase ozone levels, and methane that is over 80 times more potent greenhouse gas than CO2 in the first twenty years -- increasing temperature which then increases ozone levels ano"}
{"pair": 1023, "band": "0.60-0.70", "jaccard": 0.6562, "cosine": 0.9598, "label": "", "a_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3061", "b_id": "EPA-HQ-OAR-2021-0317-3305", "_i": 2692, "_j": 2914, "a_text": "Thank you for proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions, but it needs to be stronger. Here are some suggestions on how to strengthen these new rules: Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program.", "b_text": "Thank you for proposing a rule to reduce methane emissions, but it needs to be stronger. Please also do the following: Limit flaring of gas to only those instances in which it is necessary for safety or maintenance reasons. Strengthen the standards to address emissions from storage tanks. Provide a clear pathway for participation in the Super Emitter Response Program. Thank you for reading this."}
